  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small qualitative sample of interviews and documents from a single European capital, which may limit generalizability to other municipal governance contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzes how artificial intelligence is integrated into urban climate action in Paris, using 10 open interviews with 12 practitioners and an analysis of 13 policy documents. It maps how AI introduces incremental shifts in daily tasks, alters professional responsibilities around risk assessment, and introduces tensions between technical utility and symbolic values like accountability.",
      "why": "It reframes AI from a neutral technical tool to a socio-technical policy instrument, revealing how its deployment can depoliticize climate action and alter decision hierarchies in municipal workflows."
    }
